.Dd November 7, 2026
.Dt DQ 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dq
.Nd DescriptorQueue: file descriptors served by a user process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile the device into the kernel, place the following line in the
kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "device dq"
.Ed
.Pp
Alternatively, to load the device as a module at boot time, place the
following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dq_load="YES"
.Ed
.Pp
The module can only be loaded during boot and never unloads.
.Pp
.In dev/dq/dq.h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
device lets a process, the
.Em controller ,
mint file descriptors whose contents it serves.
A minted descriptor is an ordinary
.Vt struct file
with its own operations table and no vnode: it answers
.Xr fstat 2 ,
.Xr fstatfs 2 ,
.Xr fpathconf 2 ,
.Dv F_KINFO
and the
.Pa /dev/fd
links from an
.Em identity
the controller presented, and turns
.Xr read 2
into
.Em events
the controller reads from its
.Em queue
and answers.
The process holding such a descriptor, the
.Em requester ,
sees a regular file.
The controller never sees the requester's memory: an event carries an
offset and a length, a reply carries bytes.
.Pp
The interface is the installed header
.In dev/dq/dq.h ,
fixed width, native byte order, LP64 only and additive, and the
.Xr libdq 3
library wraps it.
This page describes the device as built in stage 1a (the
.Dv DQ_KIND_FILE
kind, read-only), and names every answer that differs from a real file.
.Ss Opening the device
.Pa /dev/dq
is created mode 0600, owner root, group wheel.
Every
.Xr open 2
runs
.Fn priv_check PRIV_DRIVER
and fails with
.Er EPERM
without that privilege, with
.Er EACCES
before that when the node's mode refuses the caller; a 32-bit process is
refused with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
In stage 1a the device permission is the authority; delivery of a
rights-limited queue descriptor through the capability plane arrives with
the
.Li BSDDescriptorQueue
provider.
.Pp
One open is one queue.
Opening
.Pa /dev/fd/ Ns Ar N
of an open queue goes through
.Xr fdescfs 4 ,
which shares the existing file rather than opening the device again, so the
new descriptor is the same queue; only a path open of
.Pa /dev/dq
creates a queue.
A controller should open the queue
.Dv O_CLOFORK
.Pq Xr libdq 3 Ap s Fn dq_open does ,
see
.Sx Delivery of a minted descriptor .
.Ss Events and replies
Every record on the queue starts with a
.Vt struct dq_event
of
.Dv DQ_API_VERSION ,
whose
.Va de_size
is the whole record, header and body; records are packed back to back in a
.Xr read 2
buffer.
.Va de_op
is the operation
.Pq Vt enum dq_op ,
.Va de_object
the object id,
.Va de_id
the reply token (0 for a record that takes no reply),
.Va de_seq
the queue's sequence number,
.Va de_caller
the requesting process (pid, parent, thread, credential, jail and
.Xr mac_capability 4
nonce;
.Dv DQ_CALLER_F_KERNEL
with pid 0 when a kernel process such as an aio daemon posted it), and
.Va de_time
the monotonic time of the post.
Stage 1a posts:
.Bl -tag -width "DQ_OP_INTERRUPT" -offset indent
.It Dv DQ_OP_READ
Body
.Vt struct dq_read_body :
.Va dr_offset
and
.Va dr_len ,
at most
.Va kern.dq.max_io .
The reply is an errno and up to
.Va dr_len
bytes; a short reply is the end of the file.
A larger
.Xr read 2
is posted as consecutive events, each waited for before the next, so the
controller sees them in order.
.It Dv DQ_OP_GETATTR
No body; posted by
.Xr fstat 2
only when the policy has
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_GETATTR .
The reply is an errno and, when it is 0, exactly one
.Vt struct dq_ident_file ,
which is validated as at mint and becomes the presented identity.
.It Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE
No body, no reply, posted at the object's last reference when the policy
has
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_CLOSE .
.It Dv DQ_OP_INTERRUPT
Body
.Vt struct dq_interrupt_body :
the
.Va din_id
of a delivered event whose requester gave up (a signal or
.Dv SIGKILL
while waiting).
No reply; the controller's own reply to that id is late.
.El
.Pp
The controller answers with
.Dv DQ_IOC_REPLY
and a
.Vt struct dq_reply :
.Va dr_id ,
.Va dr_errno
(0 or an errno) and
.Va dr_len
bytes at
.Va dr_data ,
copied before any lock is taken.
Replies may come from any thread in any order.
A reply fails with
.Er ESRCH
when the id is not in flight (never issued, already answered, abandoned,
revoked or expired; counted in
.Va ds_late_replies ) ,
and with
.Er EINVAL
for a wrong version or padding, an errno outside the kernel's range, a
length above
.Va kern.dq.max_io ,
a
.Dv DQ_OP_READ
body longer than asked or with a nonzero errno, a
.Dv DQ_OP_GETATTR
body that is not one valid identity, or a reply to an operation that takes
none.
.Ss Reading the queue
.Xr read 2
on the queue copies whole records, as many as fit, in order, and never
splits one.
A buffer smaller than a header is
.Er EINVAL ;
when the first record does not fit the call fails with
.Er ENOBUFS ;
an empty queue blocks, or answers
.Er EAGAIN
under
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
or
.Er EINTR
on a signal.
A record is taken off the queue only after its copy succeeded: a fault in
the buffer after some records were copied returns those, and a fault
before any returns
.Er EFAULT ,
with nothing lost either way.
Two threads may read one queue; each record is delivered once.
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr select 2
and
.Xr kqueue 2
.Pq Dv EVFILT_READ , with the queued bytes in Va data
report readiness.
Once the queue is closing, reads answer
.Er ENXIO ,
poll
.Dv POLLHUP
and the knote
.Dv EV_EOF .
.Pp
.Va de_seq
increases by one per posted event and is assigned before the depth test, so
a dropped event leaves a visible gap.
A queue holds
.Va kern.dq.queue_depth
unread events (a controller may lower its own queue's depth with
.Dv DQ_IOC_SET_QUEUE_DEPTH ,
never raise it); when it is full the requester's operation fails with
.Er EIO
at once and
.Va ds_events_dropped
counts it.
Before that bound a requester waits for a slot when an object has
.Va kern.dq.max_inflight_per_object
unanswered events or the requester itself has
.Va kern.dq.max_inflight_per_requester .
.Ss Waiting, deadlines and interruption
A requester waits for its reply holding only what the operation already
held (the shared offset, the object's range lock); no vnode exists.
The policy's
.Va dp_deadline_ms
bounds the wait:
.Dv DQ_DEADLINE_DEFAULT
means
.Va kern.dq.default_deadline_ms ,
0 means no bound, and a positive value is milliseconds.
A missed deadline fails the operation with
.Er EIO
and counts in
.Va ds_deadline_misses .
A signal interrupts the wait with
.Er EINTR .
Either way the event is abandoned (design 8.2): one the controller has not
yet read is withdrawn silently; one already delivered is marked
interrupted and a
.Dv DQ_OP_INTERRUPT
record names it, so a deadline that expires after delivery posts the same
record a signal does
.Pq Va ds_interrupts counts the signal case .
.Dv SIGKILL
always breaks a wait.
When a reply and a signal race, the state decides: a reply that landed
first is consumed and no interruption is recorded.
.Ss Identity
A
.Vt struct dq_ident_file
holds every field
.Xr fstat 2
returns
.Pq Va di_dev , di_ino , di_gen , di_filerev , di_mode , di_nlink , di_uid , di_gid , di_size , di_blocks , di_blksize , di_flags , di_rdev , and the four times ,
the presented path
.Pq Va di_path , di_pathlen ,
a
.Vt struct dq_statfs
with every field
.Xr fstatfs 2
returns, and a
.Vt struct dq_pathconf
whose
.Va dp_valid
bit
.Ar i
says
.Va dp_value Bq Ar i
answers the
.Dv _PC_*
name
.Va dq_pc_names Bq Ar i
(a dense index of the sparse constants, shared with userland under
.Dv DQ_WANT_PC_NAMES ) .
.Dv DQ_IOC_MINT
and
.Dv DQ_IOC_SETIDENT
validate it and refuse with
.Er EINVAL
and a reason
.Pq Vt enum dq_reason , stored through Va dm_reasonp for a mint :
.Bl -tag -width "DQ_REASON_PATHCONF" -offset indent -compact
.It Dv DQ_REASON_VERSION
.Va dm_version
is not
.Dv DQ_API_VERSION ,
or a padding field is not zero.
.It Dv DQ_REASON_KIND
the kind is not
.Dv DQ_KIND_FILE .
.It Dv DQ_REASON_PATH
.Va di_pathlen
outside 1..
.Dv DQ_PATH_MAX Ns -1,
not the string's length, or the path not absolute and normalized (an
empty,
.Ql \&.
or
.Ql ..
component, or a trailing slash).
.It Dv DQ_REASON_STRING
a statfs string without its terminating NUL.
.It Dv DQ_REASON_MNTNAME
.Va ds_mntonname
not an absolute, slash-free-at-the-end, component-boundary prefix of
.Va di_path .
.It Dv DQ_REASON_NAMEMAX
.Va ds_namemax
is 0.
.It Dv DQ_REASON_MODE
bits outside
.Dv S_IFMT
and the permission bits.
.It Dv DQ_REASON_TYPE
the
.Dv S_IFMT
bits are not the kind's
.Pq Dv S_IFREG .
.It Dv DQ_REASON_SETID
.Dv S_ISUID
or
.Dv S_ISGID
is set.
.It Dv DQ_REASON_BLKSIZE
.Va di_blksize
below the page size.
.It Dv DQ_REASON_SIZE
.Va di_size
above
.Dv OFF_MAX .
.It Dv DQ_REASON_TIME
a
.Va tv_nsec
outside 0..999999999.
.It Dv DQ_REASON_PATHCONF
.Va dp_valid
names a slot beyond
.Dv DQ_PC_NCOUNT .
.It Dv DQ_REASON_POLICY , DQ_REASON_RIGHTS
see
.Sx Policy .
.El
.Ss Policy
A
.Vt struct dq_policy
accompanies each object.
Stage 1a accepts
.Va dp_cache
.Dv DQ_CACHE_NONE
(no page cache, which is stage 1b; the only switch for whether
.Xr fstat 2
posts is
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_GETATTR )
or
.Dv DQ_CACHE_ATTR ;
.Va dp_write ,
.Va dp_exec ,
.Va dp_mmap_shared_write
and
.Va dp_ioctl
.Dv DQ_DENY ;
.Va dp_lock
.Dv DQ_EVENT
(accepted; nothing posts, see
.Sx STAGE 1A ) ;
.Va dp_deadline_ms
at least
.Dv DQ_DEADLINE_DEFAULT ;
.Va dp_events
a subset of
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_GETATTR | DQ_EVENTS_CLOSE ;
.Va dp_pad0
zero.
Anything else is
.Er EINVAL
with
.Dv DQ_REASON_POLICY .
.Va dp_rights_version
must be the
.Dv CAP_RIGHTS_VERSION
the controller built
.Va dp_rights
with, and the rights must be valid, or the reason is
.Dv DQ_REASON_RIGHTS .
The minted descriptor carries exactly
.Va dp_rights ;
a controller presenting a file as
.Xr open 2
would should pass
.Dv CAP_ALL_RIGHTS .
.Dv DQ_IOC_SETPOLICY
may later change
.Va dp_cache ,
.Va dp_deadline_ms
and
.Va dp_events
only; the other fields and the rights must equal the minted values.
.Ss Ioctls
All on the queue descriptor, group
.Ql j :
.Bl -tag -width "DQ_IOC_SET_QUEUE_DEPTH" -offset indent
.It Dv DQ_IOC_MINT Vt "struct dq_mint_args"
Mint an object of
.Va dm_kind
presenting
.Va dm_ident
under
.Va dm_policy ;
returns
.Va dm_object
and the descriptor
.Va dm_fd ,
or
.Er EINVAL
with the reason stored through
.Va dm_reasonp
when that pointer is not
.Dv NULL
(a failing ioctl copies its argument back to nobody; on success
.Dv DQ_REASON_NONE
is stored).
.Er ENOSPC
when the queue holds
.Va kern.dq.max_objects
objects,
.Er ENXIO
when it is closing.
.It Dv DQ_IOC_MINT_OBSERVER Vt "struct dq_observer_args"
Mint an observer descriptor, see
.Sx Observers .
.Er EINVAL
for a wrong version, an unknown flag or nonzero padding.
.It Dv DQ_IOC_REPLY Vt "struct dq_reply"
Answer an event; see
.Sx Events and replies .
.It Dv DQ_IOC_REVOKE Vt "struct dq_object_args"
Revoke an object: every pending and in-flight event fails with
.Er EIO ,
.Dv NOTE_REVOKE
fires on its
.Dv EVFILT_VNODE
knotes and its
.Dv EVFILT_READ
and
.Dv EVFILT_WRITE
knotes report
.Dv EV_EOF ;
later reads answer
.Er EIO
while
.Xr fstat 2 ,
.Xr fstatfs 2 ,
.Xr fpathconf 2 ,
.Dv F_KINFO ,
.Xr lseek 2
and
.Xr poll 2
keep answering from the frozen identity.
Revoking again is a no-op;
.Er ESRCH
for an id not in the table.
.It Dv DQ_IOC_SETIDENT Vt "struct dq_setident_args"
Replace the identity after validation and fire
.Dv NOTE_ATTRIB .
.Er EINVAL
without a reason,
.Er ESRCH
for an unknown object.
.It Dv DQ_IOC_SETPOLICY Vt "struct dq_setpolicy_args"
See
.Sx Policy .
.It Dv DQ_IOC_GET_STATS Vt "struct dq_stats"
The queue's counters, its kernel-wide id
.Va ds_queue_id
and its depth; also answered by an observer descriptor.
.It Dv DQ_IOC_SET_QUEUE_DEPTH Vt uint32_t
Lower the depth to 1..
.Va kern.dq.queue_depth ;
.Er EINVAL
otherwise.
.El
.Pp
.Dv FIONBIO
and
.Dv FIOASYNC
are accepted and
.Dv FIODTYPE
is answered by
.Xr devfs 4
for every character device (0 for the queue); every other command is
.Er ENOTTY .
.Ss Object lifecycle
Object ids are never reused on a queue.
An object lives until the last reference to its
.Vt struct file
is gone: descriptor table entries in every process that inherited it,
.Xr dup 2 Ns s
and
.Pa /dev/fd
reopens included.
Then its id is retired,
.Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE
is posted if asked for, and nothing waits for the controller.
The reply path holds the object's file while it runs the
.Fn mac_file_notify_reply
hook, so when the requester closed meanwhile that drop is the last
reference and the
.Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE
record then carries the controller as its caller.
When the controller closes its last descriptor to the queue, every object
is revoked, every waiter and observer woken, and later operations on the
objects answer
.Er EIO ;
.Xr fstat 2
of an object whose policy asked for
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_GETATTR
then answers from the frozen identity instead of posting.
.Ss Delivery of a minted descriptor
The minted descriptor is installed in the controller with
.Dv O_CLOEXEC ,
the policy's rights and a transfer state of
.Dv CAP_XFER_NONE :
.Xr sendmsg 2
of it over
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
fails with
.Er ENOTCAPABLE
.Pq Xr cap_xfer_limit 2 ,
while
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr fork 2
and a
.Pa /dev/fd
reopen share it.
Until the resolution stage installs served descriptors in a requester, a
requester receives one by inheritance: the controller opens its queue
.Dv O_CLOFORK ,
mints, forks, and the child holds the object and not the queue.
.Pp
A process whose descriptor table holds the queue is refused every operation
on the queue's objects that would post an event with
.Er EDEADLK
.Pq Xr read 2 , Xr pread 2 , Xr readv 2 , Xr aio_read 2 , and Xr fstat 2 under Dv DQ_EVENTS_GETATTR ;
the test is by the queue's file in the caller's table, so a
.Xr dup 2
or a
.Xr pdfork 2 Ns ed
helper that inherited it is covered and a child of an
.Dv O_CLOFORK
queue is not.
Operations the identity answers
.Pq Xr fstat 2 , Dv F_KINFO , Xr fstatfs 2 , Xr fpathconf 2 , Xr lseek 2 , Xr poll 2 , Xr kqueue 2 , Xr close 2
are never refused.
.Pp
The
.Va f_cred
of a minted file is the controller's credential, since the mint runs in
the controller; it is visible to the
.Fa file_cred
argument of the MAC file hooks and differs from a descriptor a real
.Xr open 2
returned.
The requester's own credential is what
.Va de_caller
carries.
.Ss Observers
.Dv DQ_IOC_MINT_OBSERVER
returns a read-only descriptor that receives a copy of every event posted
on the queue
.Pq flagged Dv DQ_EVENT_F_OBSERVED , with its own Va de_seq
and a
.Dv DQ_OP_REPLY
record
.Pq Vt struct dq_reply_record : id, errno, length
for every reply, with
.Va de_object
0 and a zeroed
.Va de_caller .
Lengths are always visible; data bodies are removed unless the observer
was minted with
.Dv DQ_OBS_F_DATA ,
and in stage 1a no event carries data and reply bytes belong to the
waiter, so the two forms deliver the same records.
The descriptor answers
.Xr read 2
with the queue's batching rules,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr kqueue 2
.Pq Dv EVFILT_READ , records in Va data ,
.Dv DQ_IOC_GET_STATS
and
.Xr fcntl 2
.Dv F_GETFL
and
.Dv F_SETFL
(its rights are
.Dv CAP_READ , CAP_EVENT , CAP_FSTAT , CAP_IOCTL
limited to that command and
.Dv CAP_FCNTL
limited to those two, so any other ioctl or fcntl, and
.Xr write 2 ,
are
.Er ENOTCAPABLE ) ;
it is installed
.Dv O_CLOEXEC | O_CLOFORK ,
both locked, and cannot be sent.
Copies are best effort: one that cannot be allocated or that would exceed
the depth is a counted drop with a sequence gap.
Once the queue is gone the observer drains what it holds and reads end of
file.
.Pp
The
.Xr mac_capability 4
service
.Dq dq.observe
.Pq In dev/dq/dq_observe_proto.h
lists every queue
.Pq Dv DQO_OP_LIST_QUEUES
and the objects of one
.Pq Dv DQO_OP_LIST_OBJECTS ,
and
.Dv DQO_OP_SUBSCRIBE
installs a metadata-only observer of a queue in the caller, rights-limited
and locked exactly like an ioctl-minted one, and names it in a
.Vt struct dqo_subscribe_reply
(a framework reply descriptor would carry full rights).
In stage 1a it admits a credential that may open the device.
.Sh STAGE 1A
A stage 1a object is read-only and the mutating operations answer what a
read-only descriptor on a real file answers, or the value given here.
Every row is pinned by
.Pa tests/sys/dq/dq_stage1a_test .
.Bl -tag -width "ioctl(FIOBMAP2)" -offset indent
.It Xr write 2 , Xr pwrite 2 , Xr writev 2 , Xr posix_fallocate 2 , Xr fspacectl 2
.Er EBADF :
the descriptor has no
.Dv FWRITE
and the generic layer refuses before the fileop.
.It Xr ftruncate 2
.Er EINVAL ,
the generic layer's answer for every non-writable descriptor.
.It Xr fchmod 2 , Xr fchown 2 , Xr futimens 2 , Xr fchflags 2
.Er EPERM ,
the non-owner's answer, until
.Dv SETATTR
events arrive.
.It Xr fsync 2 , Xr fdatasync 2 , Xr posix_fadvise 2
0; nothing is dirty and every valid advice is accepted.
.It Xr mmap 2
.Er ENODEV ;
the pager is stage 1b.
.It Xr aio_read 2
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
unless
.Va vfs.aio.enable_unsafe
is set:
.Xr aio 4
calls only a local vnode safe.
With it set the aio daemon posts the event, flagged
.Dv DQ_CALLER_F_KERNEL
with the requester's credential.
.It Xr sendfile 2 , Xr copy_file_range 2
.Er EINVAL .
.It ACLs, extended attributes
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.It Xr fcntl 2 record locks, Xr flock 2
.Er EBADF
and
.Er EOPNOTSUPP :
the operations table lacks
.Dv DFLAG_RECLOCK
and
.Dv DFLAG_FLOCK ,
so the generic layer refuses before any event; the accepted
.Va dp_lock
.Dv DQ_EVENT
posts nothing yet.
.It Xr fcntl 2 Dv F_READAHEAD , F_RDAHEAD , F_ISUNIONSTACK
0, as a regular file.
The kernel-private read/write hint commands reached through the Linux
compatibility layer answer 0 and
.Er EPERM .
.It Xr fcntl 2 Dv F_ADD_SEALS , F_GET_SEALS
.Er EINVAL .
.It Xr getdirentries 2
.Er ENOTDIR ,
the generic layer's answer for every non-directory.
.It Xr ioctl 2 Dv FIONREAD , FIONBIO , FIOASYNC , FIOSEEKDATA , FIOSEEKHOLE
Local: size minus offset (with the int truncation a vnode applies), 0, 0,
and one data extent covering the whole file.
.It ioctl(FIOBMAP2)
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ;
a real file runs
.Dv VOP_BMAP .
A declared difference.
.It Xr ioctl 2 , other commands
.Er ENOTTY ,
.Dv FIODTYPE
included:
.Va dp_ioctl
is
.Dv DQ_DENY .
.It Xr lseek 2
Local:
.Dv SEEK_SET ,
.Dv SEEK_CUR
and
.Dv SEEK_END
as a vnode
.Pq Er EINVAL , EOVERFLOW ;
.Dv SEEK_DATA
and
.Dv SEEK_HOLE
treat the file as one extent, so an offset at or beyond the size is
.Er ENXIO
and
.Dv SEEK_HOLE
answers the size.
.It Xr poll 2 , Xr select 2
Always ready for the standard events, as a regular file.
.It Xr kqueue 2
.Dv EVFILT_READ
reports size minus offset
.Pq ready when positive or under Dv NOTE_FILE_POLL ;
.Dv EVFILT_WRITE
is always ready with
.Va data
0;
.Dv EVFILT_VNODE
supports
.Dv NOTE_ATTRIB
.Pq Dv DQ_IOC_SETIDENT
and
.Dv NOTE_REVOKE .
.It Xr fstatfs 2
The identity's statfs, unfiltered: no mount exists for
.Fn prison_enforce_statfs
to run, so a jailed requester sees what the controller presented.
A declared difference.
.It Xr mac_get_fd 3
.Er EINVAL :
the label system calls dispatch on the file type and have no arm for a
served file.
A stage 1a difference.
.It Va f_cred
The controller's credential, see
.Sx Delivery of a minted descriptor .
.El
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
All under
.Va kern.dq ,
settable at boot and at run time:
.Bl -tag -width "max_inflight_per_requester" -offset indent -compact
.It Va max_io
Largest transfer in one event; a larger read is chunked in order (131072).
.It Va queue_depth
Unread events a queue holds before dropping (1024); the bound of
.Dv DQ_IOC_SET_QUEUE_DEPTH
and the depth of observers.
.It Va max_inflight_per_object
Unanswered events per object before requesters wait for a slot (64).
.It Va max_inflight_per_requester
Unanswered events per requester, keyed by nonce or pid (256).
.It Va max_objects
Live objects per queue (65536).
.It Va default_deadline_ms
The deadline a policy of
.Dv DQ_DEADLINE_DEFAULT
gets (30000); 0 means none.
.It Va queues
Read-only: open queues.
.El
